Istora: For example, like, checking that links are alive.

565
01:01:13.870 --> 01:01:16.780
Istora: And potentially translating into different languages.

566
01:01:17.170 --> 01:01:22.660
Istora: There's probably some uses of AI that… Basically, nobody is against.

567
01:01:24.180 --> 01:01:29.910
Istora: And I think, there might even be the case of, like.

568
01:01:31.950 --> 01:01:37.239
Istora: Turning some of the more complicated concepts into easier-to-understand articles using AI.

569
01:01:37.590 --> 01:01:42.070
Istora: As a possible… as a possible use of it.

570
01:01:45.090 --> 01:01:47.029
Istora: Do you have any opinions, Wigo?

571
01:01:48.100 --> 01:01:57.830
w1g0: Yeah, on the opinion side, regarding which, agent, will be used, and if it's

572
01:01:57.960 --> 01:02:04.559
w1g0: Freeware, or, where the funding comes from, or, well, that…

573
01:02:04.710 --> 01:02:17.430
w1g0: That's, one thing. On the other, I don't… I'm not really, in favor of, AI, because it's,

574
01:02:17.510 --> 01:02:27.209
w1g0: well, quite controversial at the moment, and as ETC is a more conservative,

575
01:02:27.800 --> 01:02:33.380
w1g0: I think, I wouldn't be ashamed to integrate that as,

576
01:02:33.830 --> 01:02:36.329
w1g0: As the last community out there.

577
01:02:38.250 --> 01:02:59.939
weder: I disagree. I think that you can be all for ETC and the principled values that it holds, while still being supportive of the use of AI. I think that human oversight is definitely the most important part, whether it comes to your developing websites or making translations, it's just important that everything gets, like, a second check

578
01:02:59.940 --> 01:03:12.260
weder: from a human, but when it comes to stuff like ETC, where the people who are contributing nowadays are volunteers, if we can triple or quadruple the productivity of the volunteers.

579
01:03:12.260 --> 01:03:26.260
weder: Instead of having to necessarily get more volunteers, it can definitely lessen the load if it can be used productively and used to simply just do things that would take you longer to do without having to take as long to do it.

580
01:03:29.170 --> 01:03:30.469
w1g0: Well, -

581
01:03:30.910 --> 01:03:44.240
w1g0: if I may, pick up on that, on… based on what, Wade regarden is saying, I could, see, use for, let's say, in the Twitter together.

582
01:03:44.240 --> 01:03:51.260
w1g0: application, an AI that is proposing, and that people are,

583
01:03:51.300 --> 01:03:54.770
w1g0: Let's say, rewriting what it says, or…

584
01:03:54.960 --> 01:04:07.859
w1g0: that approve, so that it can pass. In that, I'm… I think it can be useful, because I think that I'm not on X or Twitter or whatever.

585
01:04:07.960 --> 01:04:16.299
w1g0: But I think it can be useful because the proposed tweets were very, very low in the past.

586
01:04:17.630 --> 01:04:25.550
Istora: Right, indeed. And yeah, having even just, like, the idea generation on a… and the prompting to actually…

587
01:04:25.860 --> 01:04:28.249
Istora: Create tweets on a regular basis.

588
01:04:28.420 --> 01:04:31.330
Istora: would, I think, in itself, be useful?

589
01:04:34.740 --> 01:04:42.430
Istora: And yeah, as long as a human signs off on it, and it's not completely garbage, I don't see the negative in terms of output.

590
01:04:43.150 --> 01:04:50.340
Istora: On the front of, using AI during development, I'm totally for it, and I think that

591
01:04:50.880 --> 01:04:54.040
Istora: Like, modern development basically requires

592
01:04:54.710 --> 01:05:04.500
Istora: AI, like, almost everyone is going to be using it to some extent, some more than others, but I'm definitely in the leaning into agentic engineering camp.

593
01:05:04.760 --> 01:05:10.590
Istora: And if I do work on the website, then that will most certainly be using coding agents,

594
01:05:11.070 --> 01:05:15.720
Istora: To help with the migration, even if it's not, like… in the production

595
01:05:16.240 --> 01:05:25.099
Istora: side of having, like, a chatbot to talk to ETC or something, but during the development rollout, I'm… I'm definitely pro-AI in that… in that sense.

596
01:05:25.740 --> 01:05:28.910
Istora: As long as there's, like, a level of…

597
01:05:29.330 --> 01:05:37.900
Istora: quality to the work that's outputted, and, you know, like any tool, it can be abused. So, as long as that's kept in check, then…

598
01:05:39.050 --> 01:05:46.560
Istora: like Wiedergarden is saying, it's something that can really help amplify the efforts of the volunteers.

599
01:05:47.150 --> 01:05:53.880
Istora: And, with a limited number of volunteers, I think it would be more and more essential for us to lean into these tools to have an effect.

600
01:05:55.050 --> 01:05:59.430
w1g0: I think, I think I already said that I'm not…

601
01:05:59.800 --> 01:06:03.599
w1g0: much in favor of AI, to…

602
01:06:03.800 --> 01:06:09.839
w1g0: implemented really quickly, because I'm more inclined to,

603
01:06:10.160 --> 01:06:13.560
w1g0: Search other volunteers and have a human.

604
01:06:13.710 --> 01:06:17.520
w1g0: Interaction, with everything.

605
01:06:17.720 --> 01:06:35.790
w1g0: And this is based, because I'm, yeah, I'm more an observer, I'm more an analyst, and from the contributions I see, they are very, very low quality, to begin with, and secondly, it's always a human that needs to check.

606
01:06:35.880 --> 01:06:43.409
w1g0: And, that's… while that's great for beginners, I have the impression that, senior,

607
01:06:43.680 --> 01:06:52.449
w1g0: The seniors, really get annoyed by, the low quality, and that they'd rather do it themselves from the first,

608
01:06:53.530 --> 01:06:54.980
w1g0: From the first time.

609
01:06:54.980 --> 01:07:05.500
weder: I think that's an interesting position, but I see it the other way around. The way I see it is that developers who've been doing development for before… since before there was AI,

610
01:07:05.500 --> 01:07:19.889
weder: they understand how to work a codebase, they can do that by hand. They don't need AI. So when they… when AI steps into the picture, I feel like… and you may not have experienced this yourself, but at least for me.

611
01:07:20.070 --> 01:07:31.039
weder: what happened was, is that it essentially took over certain parts of things that would… the time-consuming parts of things that I would typically not exactly enjoy the most doing.

612
01:07:31.040 --> 01:07:42.430
weder: are effectively stripped out, and it's replaced by my, essentially, mostly my oversight, of what's going on. And I… and I'm not sure about the future, but

613
01:07:42.430 --> 01:07:47.129
weder: The way things are moving right now, and it depends on you, you know, how you see things.

614
01:07:47.290 --> 01:07:49.500
weder: AI is…

615
01:07:50.180 --> 01:08:10.039
weder: becoming something that if you don't adapt to, then you may fall behind, and you may not, you may not be seen as adapt as people who are both good at development, and also good with artificial intelligence to help them. And the people who are just starting to learn how to code now.

616
01:08:10.040 --> 01:08:35.010
weder: they are at a disadvantage because they are used to having artificial intelligence there, and they're not used to being able to debug things and to do things manually, and they are going to learn from the start to rely on AI to fix those things. So, from your point, I agree that it does cause quality issues, but I would say that comes from the more newer developed

617
01:08:35.010 --> 01:08:52.549
weder: developers, and it's more important that we just try to expand, to give our knowledge from before AI and show them that you have to learn the ropes a little bit yourself before you incorporate AI, or else you're not going to understand what's going on in the back end and what's going on behind the scenes.

618
01:08:55.279 --> 01:09:10.329
w1g0: Yeah, that makes perfect sense, but in the same way, Ethereum Classic is not the, the apex chain out there, so, maybe the timing is not,

619
01:09:10.849 --> 01:09:12.689
w1g0: so crucial.

620
01:09:16.260 --> 01:09:26.329
Istora: Yeah, I think Wiedergarden, you hit the nail on the head there, and I agree with your analysis. In terms of the timing for ETC,

621
01:09:26.680 --> 01:09:36.930
Istora: I'd say, like, really, we just need to get things done, and if these tools are helping us achieve that with the limited resources we have, then that's a good thing.

622
01:09:37.319 --> 01:09:46.999
Istora: I actually look forward, Wego, to showing you what is possible, with These agentic engineering tools.

623
01:09:47.130 --> 01:09:54.599
Istora: in the right hands, and I'm thinking, like, the amount of hours I spent on the current version of the website just doing…

624
01:09:56.220 --> 01:10:10.729
Istora: complete monkey work, basically, aligning divs, etc, is something that now takes seconds instead of hours and hours, and that time saved can be going to other things and more productive things, so…

625
01:10:10.980 --> 01:10:16.340
Istora: Yeah, I… all I'd ask is keep an open mind, and…

626
01:10:16.700 --> 01:10:24.769
Istora: Let's, work closely, and hopefully you'll be able to see some of the power of this technology, if we're working together on the website.

627
01:10:25.270 --> 01:10:28.630
Istora: At least in terms of the… the less essential stuff.

628
01:10:29.040 --> 01:10:39.860
Istora: And when it comes to the core clients, this is where it's really an important question of how much AI is too much AI, and what kind of quality controls are necessary.

629
01:10:40.020 --> 01:10:46.499
Istora: Because it's not just a website, it's not just a contract even, but it's the whole protocol.

630
01:10:47.090 --> 01:10:55.859
Diego: Yeah, I think, when… I mean, my opinion in this is about curation of the content. That's, the most important thing.

631
01:10:56.150 --> 01:10:59.340
Diego: And it will also depend on how…

632
01:10:59.470 --> 01:11:04.030
Diego: large is the codebase that we are building on, like, for example.

633
01:11:04.150 --> 01:11:11.580
Diego: The codebase for the Nethermine plugin is less than 2,000 lines of code.

634
01:11:12.140 --> 01:11:19.050
Diego: Which will be quite sim… quite easily reviewable for any human being,

635
01:11:19.170 --> 01:11:21.549
Diego: And I think that's the way to go.

636
01:11:21.800 --> 01:11:28.300
Diego: For larger code bases, it may be, like, a challenge itself.

637
01:11:28.500 --> 01:11:40.030
Diego: like, for example, when I… something that I did when I've been working on, well, GETC, so this new type of revamped version of CoreGet on top of Core Ethereum.

638
01:11:40.560 --> 01:11:42.929
Diego: Was to… to build.

639
01:11:43.290 --> 01:11:44.830
Diego: diff basis.

640
01:11:44.980 --> 01:11:55.420
Diego: So, because the main idea with that work was to try to bring it back, all the changes that were removed by the GoEthereum team.

641
01:11:56.240 --> 01:12:02.569
Diego: All the things that were important for a proof-of-work network, and adapting what was necessary

642
01:12:02.720 --> 01:12:13.269
Diego: for… for, like, adapting into their new architecture, because, of course, they have been changing things. But what… what I tried was to… well, to… to…

643
01:12:13.730 --> 01:12:19.100
Diego: Turn back to life to those, those, commits.

644
01:12:19.330 --> 01:12:25.820
Diego: So, kind of reverting them, but in an intelligent way. And during that process, what it did was

645
01:12:26.150 --> 01:12:32.539
Diego: To put in place several tools that will create a wiki page with the actual diff.

646
01:12:32.850 --> 01:12:36.280
Diego: That will allow anyone to check

647
01:12:36.540 --> 01:12:39.340
Diego: If anything had been changed from

648
01:12:39.640 --> 01:12:43.329
Diego: The previous state of the codebase.

649
01:12:43.490 --> 01:12:45.820
Diego: So, that… will not…

650
01:12:45.970 --> 01:12:56.850
Diego: Ensure that, it will be bug-free, but at least it should give us a good signal that it will be in the very same state.

651
01:12:57.080 --> 01:12:58.949
Diego: As before the removals.

652
01:12:59.150 --> 01:13:07.559
Diego: So, that's where I think it's important, the curation and all the tools that we can build around for humans to validate

653
01:13:07.700 --> 01:13:09.269
Diego: what the AI made.

654
01:13:09.580 --> 01:13:14.609
Diego: And… and more… moreover, in… in a consensus.

655
01:13:14.800 --> 01:13:16.410
Diego: Client.

656
01:13:16.620 --> 01:13:26.789
Diego: So, yes, I think that's… that's important, and I agree on both sides. I think it's an amazing tool that allows us to do amazing things.

657
01:13:26.930 --> 01:13:33.290
Diego: But the human touch and the eye and the criteria has to still be there.

658
01:13:33.550 --> 01:13:38.350
Diego: And we never had to lose that, at least, for now. Yeah.

659
01:13:38.350 --> 01:13:41.169
Istora: Right. And whilst the AI can…

660
01:13:41.770 --> 01:13:43.959
Istora: Create valid code.

661
01:13:44.280 --> 01:13:54.570
Istora: it doesn't necessarily have the depth and the historical context to understand what's best for ETC, specifically. I mean, for example, this…

662
01:13:55.170 --> 01:14:01.320
Istora: this previous topic of F… F… 69. 69?

663
01:14:01.660 --> 01:14:14.450
Istora: It could be the case that maybe an agent decided, okay, this is… let's upgrade, you know? You need to bump the version. And then you just go down that path and spend however many hours implementing that without really under, like, should we do this?

664
01:14:14.720 --> 01:14:21.320
Istora: And that's… that's not something that I think that AI is at yet, to make those high-context decisions.

665
01:14:21.440 --> 01:14:27.710
Diego: Yes, part of my research was also, like, trying to… to go deep into the… the…

666
01:14:27.950 --> 01:14:32.570
Diego: Earlier on the old decisions that lead to the inclusion of

667
01:14:32.870 --> 01:14:42.589
Diego: of this, and then was when I realized, okay, yeah, that was there for a reason, and they already had this discussion internally into the Ethereum

668
01:14:43.180 --> 01:14:49.749
Diego: groups. So, yeah, that was already settled. But it's, yeah, that's something that, yeah, the AI missed.

669
01:14:50.050 --> 01:14:50.760
Diego: Probably.

670
01:14:51.340 --> 01:14:53.049
Istora: Could be the case. Yup.

671
01:14:53.900 --> 01:14:56.630
Istora: And, and yeah, the… heh.

672
01:14:57.030 --> 01:15:07.669
Istora: It could be the case that in the far future, like, the whole protocol is controlled by agents, but I really don't think we're there yet, and we have to recognize the limitations of the tools that exist as they do today.

673
01:15:08.130 --> 01:15:12.700
Istora: And I see them as just… Like, extremely good code completion.

674
01:15:13.200 --> 01:15:16.690
Istora: But not necessarily, decision-making.

675
01:15:17.140 --> 01:15:18.050
Istora: Tools.

676
01:15:18.250 --> 01:15:26.530
Istora: And depending on the complexity and importance of the software that's been worked on, I think…

677
01:15:26.670 --> 01:15:32.290
Istora: humans definitely still have a large part to play. Yeah. And we'll do for the foreseeable future.

678
01:15:34.570 --> 01:15:41.120
Diego: Yeah, something like, for example, that an AI is… well, in my experience, has not been incredible, was that…

679
01:15:41.260 --> 01:15:53.519
Diego: Like, when you're working, for example, in a large codebase written by humans like BESU, and you want to introduce a refactor that has to be readable for reviewers, that you have to convince to include it.

680
01:15:53.650 --> 01:15:59.239
Diego: It's not really good, because it doesn't think in the same way that a human does.

681
01:15:59.420 --> 01:16:15.799
Diego: So, you have to kind of manually go through all the transformation, probably to reach out the proposition that it made, but you have to do it in a way that will convince, and during the process, you will get convinced also that it's,

682
01:16:15.890 --> 01:16:20.919
Diego: It could change, like, taking all the guardrails that you will…

683
01:16:21.060 --> 01:16:31.300
Diego: take for any call that you had written manually, so I think, like, for example, that's… it's another example where the human touch is really important, because in the end.

684
01:16:31.650 --> 01:16:36.739
Diego: There are still humans merging code, and you have to think of them.

685
01:16:37.150 --> 01:16:45.730
Diego: And the best usage of their time as reviewers and maintainers of the codebase. So it's not just about, throwing up, like.

686
01:16:46.070 --> 01:16:54.380
Diego: a pull request with a thousand lines changing, because, I mean, that will be actually disrespectful for the time of the reviewers.

687
01:16:54.710 --> 01:17:03.910
Diego: So yeah, that's also something else that we have to keep an eye on. Right, right. And actually, it was a time that I was talking with a Nethermine team.

688
01:17:04.200 --> 01:17:08.800
Diego: About some pull requests that I had to send for… for the…

689
01:17:09.110 --> 01:17:15.229
Diego: ETC plugin to work well with their codebase, and they told me that I had to, like.

690
01:17:15.410 --> 01:17:25.150
Diego: signal to them to send them a message with a pull request, because they were receiving that many pull requests coming from AI that was impossible for them to handle, and…

691
01:17:25.370 --> 01:17:31.690
Diego: As they are, like, really good professionals, they had to… to go through all the… all the proposals.

692
01:17:31.810 --> 01:17:47.120
Diego: But, yeah, most of them were garbage or on this shape. I mean, someone ran an agent against their codebase, and who knows which prompt they use, and okay, they decide to just send a pull request.

693
01:17:47.470 --> 01:17:52.670
Diego: So, yeah, so the human part there is important. Right.

694
01:17:52.670 --> 01:17:57.490
Istora: And unless you have experience working with the codebase and the protocol.

695
01:17:57.630 --> 01:18:08.430
Diego: Yeah, and also having experience reviewing other people's code and all of that. So, yeah, I think that's still the human part. It's there, and it's quite important.

696
01:18:08.430 --> 01:18:14.130
Istora: Right. So even if it's easy to generate a ton of new features and have

697
01:18:14.950 --> 01:18:17.539
Istora: I don't know, like, 10 pull requests a day.

698
01:18:17.650 --> 01:18:19.480
Istora: Through agentic work.

699
01:18:20.460 --> 01:18:27.250
Istora: that doesn't actually improve the speed at which you can merge them. Yeah. You still need the human in the loop there.

700
01:18:27.540 --> 01:18:34.520
Istora: And this slow, methodical, Human-based approach, especially for the critical

701
01:18:34.830 --> 01:18:40.069
Istora: lines of code in the clients, I think is going to be important to keep around for a long time.

702
01:18:44.140 --> 01:18:49.169
Istora: Okay, so we are, like… it's been a good discussion, it's been…

703
01:18:49.330 --> 01:18:56.470
Istora: more than an hour now, so I think maybe now's a good time to wrap things up. Would…

704
01:18:56.920 --> 01:19:04.300
Istora: Is there any other comments or last things to mention before we do so from… from… to join us?

705
01:19:06.780 --> 01:19:19.370
w1g0: I have, nothing else to add. I just, wanted to, ask you what the counter… the ECIP for the counter-proposal for Olympia, was.

706
01:19:20.100 --> 01:19:22.969
Istora: That number was 1120.

707
01:19:24.930 --> 01:19:26.529
Diego: Awesome. Chocolate.

708
01:19:28.150 --> 01:19:33.140
Istora: ECIP 1120. I'll add that to the show notes, which you'll find in the transcript.

709
01:19:33.140 --> 01:19:33.830
w1g0: Thank you.

710
01:19:34.940 --> 01:19:35.740
Istora: No problem.

711
01:19:38.080 --> 01:19:40.110
weder: Nothing for me, thank you so much.

712
01:19:41.840 --> 01:19:46.520
Istora: Okay, thank you both for joining, and…

713
01:19:47.150 --> 01:20:04.700
Istora: The next community call will be 2 weeks in the future, probably at the regular time, sorry, Europe, of 0 to 100 hours UTC on Friday, 2 weeks' time. So, thank you for everyone participating today. It's been a…

714
01:20:05.080 --> 01:20:07.780
Istora: A deeply stimulating call.

715
01:20:07.910 --> 01:20:14.710
Istora: So, yeah, see you next time. Thanks for joining. Till then, stay classy.

716
01:20:14.710 --> 01:20:15.810
Diego: Thank you.

717
01:20:16.030 --> 01:20:17.939
w1g0: Thanks for the discussion. Bye.
